WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.540
25 million adults and children suffering from labor and sex trafficking

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:05.580
all over the world.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:10.440
The FBI says that around 300,00 children could be at risk.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.760
Even 1 woman or 1 child trafficked is too many.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.740
But there are thousands and thousands and thousands.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.860
How many children are sex trafficked every year in the United States?

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.540
The numbers repeated by politicians in the media

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:25.020
are almost never representative of the truth.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.260
That's because studying this population presents an enormous challenge for researchers.

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.360
When underage people are involved in the sex trade in any way,

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.660
everyone around them is doing anything they can to conceal what's happening.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.960
Even so, one statistic has permeated the anti child-sex trafficking movement.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.520
It was first widely circulated in 2011,

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.480
when activists were trying to build a case against Backpage.com,

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.320
the online classified site which is now the focus of an upcoming criminal trial.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.820
It was around this time that a claim reappeared in the media

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:57.040
that 100,000 to 300,000 underage people were sex trafficked each year in the United States.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.700
It turns out that claim was bogus.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.760
Still the number was popularized, by Ashton Kutcher and Demi Moore,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.960
in their Real Men Don't Buy Sex campaign.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.600
There are more slaves in the world today,

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.740
then ever before in human history.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:11.680
It's happening in the United States,

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.400
there's between 100 and 300 thousand child sex slaves in the United States today.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.980
The number ran everywhere.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.080
It ran in the New York Times, it ran in USA Today, in CNN.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.500
Every damn media outlet took and accepted this

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.040
without ever looking at the source material.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.740
That Michael Lacey a former owner of Village Voice media

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.180
which owned Backpage.com at the time.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.900
A cover story in the Village Voice revealed that the 100,000 to 300,000 number

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.700
that was being talked about was being taken out of context.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.220
I just had my writer specifically look at this University of Pennsylvania story.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.740
That's where this 100,000-300,000 number comes from

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.220
his writers traced it back to a 2001 journal article

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.140
estimating that there were 100,000-300,000 children

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:54.180
deemed at risk for sexual exploitation in the U.S., Canada, and Mexico,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.040
not just the United States, as Kutcher and others had claimed.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.140
That would be 6,000 underage children

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.980
being sex trafficked in America's 50 largest cities.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:05.720
It was totally junk science.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.300
The Voice also found out that the study's methodology was flawed,

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:10.560
because it's figure weren't based on actual cases.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.460
The authors relied on guesses as to who might end up a sex trafficking victim.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.180
And the data was more than 12 years out of date by the time Kutcher and others were citing it.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:21.220
Even the Crimes Against Children Research Center at the University of New Hampshire

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.980
advised journalists and researchers in 2008 not to cite this study,

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.840
saying scientifically credible estimates do not exist.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.340
The study's lead authors disavowed it as well.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.820
After this story appeared in the Village Voice criticizing Kutcher's activism,

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:38.200
Kutcher took to Twitter calling on advertisers to stop buying ads in the Village Voice.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.000
It's like a bar fight, you know?

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.760
And you just can't see where the next idiot is going to come from,

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.140
you just know that they're coming, okay?

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.000
And it's like a bar fight that never ends.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.780
The 100,000-300,000 number still pops up in online stories and newspaper op-eds,

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.920
and is often attributed to dated government publications.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.100
Though as the junk science number still gets cited,

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.240
a world where we truly understand the reality of all the types of people

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.600
that make up the sex trade still escapes us.

